---
// A cow of butter, every summer since 1911, and the woman who made it for 46 years.
---

<p>
	I guess in 1911 there was no Metaverse and no celebrity deepfakes to pass one's time with, so a life-size cow out of butter must have been quite the spectacle.
</p>
<figure class="milk-story-picture">
	<img src="/assets/milk/stories/butter-cow.webp" width="800" height="532" alt="The butter cow at the Iowa State Fair, in its cooler" loading="lazy" />
	<figcaption>Photo: Iowa State Fair</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>
	Like, 1911 is tangible timewise, you know? It's the year my grandma was born. They had no electricity, and the coolest thing in your week was probably washing your bedsheets or whatever.
</p>
<p>
	But then you walk into the Iowa State fair, and there you see it... a cow, made entirely of butter.
</p>
<p>
	A cow made from dairy! That's so creative, so self-referential. It is working man's art, truly. And then everyone goes all lavish and eats a lot and celebrates the abundance and has a good time.
</p>
<p>
	In 2026 it doesn't hit quite the same as it did in 1911, but it remains a fun oddity, and I appreciate the tradition. It kinda makes me want to visit Iowa and see it in person.
</p>
